<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kashgar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kashi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kashgar is an oasis city in the far west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, near the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> routes across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a> met those around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>. For centuries a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> Muslim culture and trade, it was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Karakhanids</a>, the Chagatai khans and the Khoja leaders, paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> from 1680 and was conquered by the Qing in 1759.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kashgar.jpg" alt="Kashgar" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: N509FZ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H%C3%ABytgah_Mosque,_Kashi_(20230923100109).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kashgar, also called Kashi in Chinese, lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>. For over 2,000 years it has been a strategically important oasis on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> linking China with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It stands at the convergence of widely different cultures and empires and has been under Chinese, Turkic, Mongol and Tibetan rule. Its earliest recorded names are Shufu and Shule, and Ptolemy calls it Kasi. The name Kashgar did not appear in Chinese records until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest mention of Kashgar comes from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> envoy who travelled the Northern Silk Road. In 76 BCE the Chinese conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, Khotan, Kashgar and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> states. Its people practised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. In the Tang period a long struggle began between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the Western Turks for the basin, and the king of Kashgar sent emissaries to the Tang capital in 635 and 639. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> invaded in 711, but Islam was established at Kashgar only in the 10th century, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> swept away the Kara-Khitai in 1219, and Kashgar then came under the Chagatai Khans. Marco Polo visited the city and recorded numerous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Nestorian Christians</a> with their own churches. In the 14th century the Chagataid khan Tughluq Timur converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The Qing defeated the Dzungar Khanate and took control of Kashgar in 1759, settling emigrants of other ethnic groups near a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> garrison. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dungan-revolt">Dungan Revolt</a> of 1862 to 1877 reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, and in 1864 the people of Kashgar rose against their rulers and called on the Kyrgyz chief Sadik Beg, who was reinforced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buzurg-khan">Buzurg Khan</a> and his general Yakub Beg.</p><p>From 1912 to 1928 Yang Zengxin governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, and the Republic created Shule County at Kashgar in 1913. The British consulate at Chini Bagh watched local developments. In September 1949 the provincial authorities declared their allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>, and units of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> entered southern Xinjiang, including Kashgar, from October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kashgar is a county-level city and the administrative centre of Kashgar Prefecture, which had about 4 million people in 2010. The city had 711,300 inhabitants in 2019. In 1986 the Chinese government designated it a city of historical and cultural significance. It is a terminus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakoram-highway">Karakoram Highway</a>. It has a cool arid climate and is one of the driest cities in the world, with about 85 millimetres of precipitation a year.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kashgar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1680 the cities of Altishahr, among them <b>Kashgar</b>, Yarkand and Khotan, paid the Dzungars substantial tribute in grain, cloth and silver.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From Alexandria Eschate the Greco-Bactrians may have led expeditions as far as <b>Kashgar</b> and Ürümqi, the first known contacts between China and the West around 220 BCE, and Strabo wrote that they extended their empire as far as the Seres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The early rulers, who held Balasagun in the Chu valley and <b>Kashgar</b> in the western Tarim Basin, are known mainly from later Muslim traditions and from coins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Repelled at <b>Kashgar</b> in 1131, he extended his authority over Qayaliq and Almaliq, and in 1134 took the Karakhanid city of Balasaghun, which became his capital; the Kankalis, Karluks, Kyrgyz and the Kingdom of Qocho became vassals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Malik Shah I (r. 1072–1092) the empire campaigned across Syria, Anatolia, the Caucasus and Central Asia and received the submission of the Karakhanids of Samarkand and <b>Kashgar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the west, the Karakhanids, who ruled <b>Kashgar</b> and the lands around it from the 10th century, became the first Turkic dynasty to adopt Islam, and their language, close to Old Turkic, became a language of Islamic literature written in Arabic script.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-turkic">Old Turkic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
